<div class="page-title"><h1>Applications</h1></div>
<div class="prose">

<p>Microplotter systems serve two core application areas: printed electronics and life-science research. Both rely on the same ultrasonic dispensing platform &mdash; only the ink and the substrate change.</p>

<p class="jumplinks"><a href="#applications">Applications</a> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <a href="#publications">Publications</a></p>

<h2 id="applications">Applications</h2>

<h3>Printed electronics</h3>

<p>Print conductive traces and features directly onto a substrate for microelectronic prototyping and sensor development, including:</p>

<ul>
<li>Nanometallic inks &mdash; silver, gold, copper</li>
<li>Carbon nanotubes and graphene</li>
<li>Conductive and insulating polymer solutions</li>
</ul>

<p>Feature widths are limited by dispenser aperture and substrate wettability &mdash; feature sizes as small as 5 &micro;m have been achieved with tuning, and 20&ndash;30 &micro;m is commonly achievable with most solutions. The Microplotter Pro's 5 &micro;m positioning resolution makes it the recommended system for higher-precision electronics prototyping.</p>

<h3>Life sciences</h3>

<p>Deposit biological solutions with the same precision, for applications including:</p>

<ul>
<li>Protein and oligonucleotide microarrays</li>
<li>Genomic DNA spotting</li>
<li>ELISA-type assay preparation</li>
<li>Mass spectrometry sample prep</li>
</ul>

<h3>Material &amp; substrate range</h3>

<p>Any material compatible with standard inkjet technology can be dispensed with a Microplotter system, up to 450 cP viscosity (some higher-viscosity materials with good wetting properties can also be loaded). Particle sizes must be half the dispenser tip's inside diameter (1&ndash;70 &micro;m), with best performance under 5 &micro;m. Solvents must be no more volatile than toluene. See <a href="{{ '/materials/' | url }}">Materials</a> for our ink partners.</p>

<p>Printing works on any planar surface, or any flexible material that can be laid flat &mdash; including silicon, glass, quartz, stainless steel, and a wide range of polymers.</p>

